# Project 4: Microsoft Entra ID and Azure RBAC

For this project, I used Azure CLI to create a Microsoft Entra security group, add my user to it, and give the group read access to a dedicated Azure resource group.

I wanted to understand how Entra ID and Azure RBAC work together. Entra ID holds users and groups. Azure RBAC controls what those identities can do with Azure resources.

## What I set up

- A temporary resource group named `rg-rbac-lab-<timestamp>` in `centralus`
- An Entra security group named `grp-azure-rbac-readers-<timestamp>`
- My signed-in user as a member of that group
- The built-in `Reader` role assigned to the group at the lab resource-group scope

I used a separate, empty resource group for the exercise, so I didn’t need to deploy a VM or other compute resources.

## How I did it

First, I checked the active Azure subscription and retrieved my Entra user object ID:

```bash
az account show --output table
export SUBSCRIPTION_ID="$(az account show --query id --output tsv)"
export USER_ID="$(az ad signed-in-user show --query id --output tsv)"
```

Then I created the lab resource group and set its RBAC scope:

```bash
export LAB_SUFFIX="$(date +%Y%m%d%H%M%S)"
export RG="rg-rbac-lab-${LAB_SUFFIX}"

az group create \
  --name "$RG" \
  --location centralus \
  --tags Project=AzureRBACLab Environment=Training

export SCOPE="/subscriptions/${SUBSCRIPTION_ID}/resourceGroups/${RG}"
```

I created the security group and saved its object ID:

```bash
export GROUP_NAME="grp-azure-rbac-readers-${LAB_SUFFIX}"
export MAIL_NICKNAME="azrbareaders${LAB_SUFFIX}"

export GROUP_ID="$(az ad group create \
  --display-name "$GROUP_NAME" \
  --mail-nickname "$MAIL_NICKNAME" \
  --description "Temporary security group for Azure RBAC training" \
  --query id \
  --output tsv)"
```

Next, I added my user and assigned the group the `Reader` role at the resource group scope:

```bash
az ad group member add \
  --group "$GROUP_ID" \
  --member-id "$USER_ID"

az role assignment create \
  --assignee-object-id "$GROUP_ID" \
  --assignee-principal-type Group \
  --role Reader \
  --scope "$SCOPE"
```

I verified membership and the role assignment with `az ad group member check`, `az ad group member list`, and `az role assignment list`. I also checked the group and assignment in the Azure portal under **Microsoft Entra ID → Groups** and **Resource group → Access control (IAM)**.

## What I took away

The role assignment has three parts: **who** gets access (the group), **what** they can do (`Reader`), and **where** it applies (the resource group). Scoping it to the lab resource group kept the exercise separate from the rest of the subscription.

A `Reader` assignment doesn’t take away access granted somewhere else. If a user already has `Contributor` or `Owner` through another assignment, those permissions still apply. To test a true read-only boundary, I’d need a separate user without other direct or inherited roles.

I also reviewed the difference between `Reader`, `Contributor`, and `Owner`. Contributor can manage resources but cannot assign Azure roles; Owner can do both. For VM-specific management, a narrower role such as `Virtual Machine Contributor` may be a better fit than general Contributor.

## Cleanup

I removed the role assignment, deleted the Entra group, and deleted the empty resource group:

```bash
az role assignment delete \
  --assignee "$GROUP_ID" \
  --role Reader \
  --scope "$SCOPE"

az ad group delete --group "$GROUP_ID"
az group delete --name "$RG" --yes

az group exists --name "$RG"
```

The last command should return `false` after the resource group deletion finishes.

## Permissions I needed

The Azure subscription permissions and Entra directory permissions are separate. Creating the resource group and role assignment requires Azure permissions at the relevant scope. Creating a group and managing its members requires suitable Entra directory permissions. Being an Azure subscription Owner doesn’t automatically grant permission to create Entra groups.

